  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The remnants of Hurricane Isaias will initially \nbring some gusty downsloping winds off the Chautauqua Ridge and then \na soaking rain to the region tonight through Monday morning.\n\nRidging at the surface and aloft will gradually exit east across \nwestern and north central New York as the remnants of Hurricane of \nIsaias becomes vertically-stacked as it passes through the trough \naloft into the mid-Atlantic Seaboard region. Isolated rain showers \nwill be possible after midnight tonight, especially across the \nwestern Southern Tier, due to moist isentropic lift aloft generated \nby the low pressure system. Additionally tonight ahead of the \nsurface low, southeast winds across the Chautauqua Ridge will \nsupport increased downslope flow across the I-90 corridor of \nChautauqua county. Wind gusts will gust up to 35 and 40 mph. \n\nHeading into Sunday, the low will  pass northeastward from the lower \nOhio Valley toward the central Appalachians, while the trough axis \nnears the forecast area, supporting widespread steady rain (heavy at \ntimes), to overspread the region from south to north as isentropic \nlift aloft continues across the region and taps into the plume of \nmoisture originating from the southern Gulf Stream.\n\nThe trough axis aloft will then pass from the Great Lakes to the \nAppalachians and further toward the Canadian Maritimes Sunday night \nthrough Monday. Meanwhile the vertically stacked surface low will \ndissipate over the Ohio Valley Sunday, while a new surface low \ndevelops over the Delmarva Peninsula and deepen while moving \nnortheastward toward southern New England Monday. Additionally \nMonday, a surface ridge of high pressure will build across the \neastern Great Lakes. Overall this will act to taper the rain from \nwest to east Sunday night into early Monday morning, with \npredominantly dry weather expected for the remainder of Monday.\n\nCurrent forecasted rainfall amounts for Sunday through Sunday night \nrange from a half of an inch to near three-quarters of an inch,\nwith the higher amounts occurring across southern New York.",
